            "text": "After the rain, the city seems to remember that it was built from things that once belonged to the earth. Brick darkens into clay. Wooden doors swell against their frames. In the cracks of the pavement, small green lives stand upright, looking less like intruders than tenants returning from holiday.\n\nI like this brief rearrangement of authority. The traffic lights still give orders, but their reflections tremble in puddles, and nobody can make them stop. A bus passes wearing a blurred red ribbon of itself. Somewhere, water drips from a fire escape with the patience of an extremely old clock.\n\nPeople emerge cautiously, then all at once. Umbrellas close like tired birds. A delivery rider shakes one sleeve and laughs at something through an earbud. Two strangers negotiate a narrow patch of dry sidewalk, performing that little dance in which each person briefly becomes the obstacle and then the solution.\n\nNothing important has happened, which is perhaps why everything feels available for notice. The bakery window glows. A dog examines a leaf with professional seriousness. Above the rooftops, the clouds begin to separate, revealing a blue so pale it looks newly invented, not yet tested against the weight of weather.\n\nSoon the streets will dry, and the city will put its familiar expression back on. We will hurry again. But for another minute, there is this: a world rinsed clean of its own explanations, asking for nothing more elaborate than our attention, and giving us somewhere beautiful to place it."
          }
        ],
